\chapter{Sources, Editions, and Historical Method}
\label{app:sources}

\section{What the source collection can establish}

The supplied collection is rich but uneven. It includes historical papers,
textbooks, manuals, memoirs, OCR transcriptions, translated editions,
duplicate files, empty files, and short fragments. Its filenames are useful
locators but not reliable bibliographic descriptions. This manuscript uses
contents and internal title pages to identify works.

The bibliography's local-file notes provide an audit trail.
A companion \texttt{SOURCE-GUIDE.md} in the repository maps citation keys
to exact files and records limitations. The guide also distinguishes
supplementary official project documentation from the local collection.
The sources themselves have been left unchanged.

Different kinds of evidence answer different questions.
A contemporaneous design paper can establish what its authors described
at that date. A memoir can explain motivations and social context,
while being subject to retrospective selection. A textbook can provide
a coherent technical model that is intentionally simpler than every
shipping implementation. A source tree can establish what code does only
when interpreted with its build, hardware, and version context.

\section{The historical backbone}

Ritchie's ``Evolution'' is the main account of the early development
sequence. It explicitly dates Unix's birth to 1969, distinguishes earlier
experiments from the published 1974 description, and gives concrete context
for patent work, pipes, and C. The version in the collection identifies
an original 1979 presentation and a 1984 journal reprint. The bibliography
cites the latter while preserving that distinction \parencite{evolution}.

Ritchie and Thompson's time-sharing-system paper provides the public
technical snapshot. It is used for its file and process model and for
what the authors said about the system's applications at that moment.
Thompson's 1978 implementation paper provides a later architectural
account, including a filesystem layout that should not be projected
backward onto every earlier edition
\parencite{ritchie-thompson,implementation}.

Kernighan's memoir supplies context about Bell Labs, the C/A/T,
tools, commercialization, and descendants. The supplied copy's copyright
page says 2020, so the bibliography uses that year rather than silently
substituting another edition's date. Salus supplies a complementary
history of users, licensing, GNU, BSD, and Linux. The supplied Salus
compilation has no securely established publication date, so none has
been invented \parencite{kernighan-history,salus}.

The short file named as a quarter-century Unix history is not treated as
a complete copy of Salus's separate book. Similarly, the file named
\path{ken-thompson-history-of-unix.txt} contains Markus Kuhn's 2003
Unix Tools lectures. It is not cited as a first-person Thompson history.
Correct attribution takes precedence over a convenient filename.

\section{The implementation backbone}

Lions's supplied commentary is the 1977 teaching text about Sixth Edition.
The explanations in this book use its chapter and section structure,
especially processes, switching, critical sections, file tables, and
inodes. They do not reproduce its source listing or claim that its
techniques constitute modern production guidance \parencite{lions}.

The supplied xv6 text is the RISC-V edition dated 27 October 2019.
Its page tables, trap entry, locking, scheduling, and log are treated as
a coherent comparison implementation. Section numbers refer to that
edition. Online xv6 revisions can differ, so readers using another version
should locate the corresponding topic rather than assume a line number
will match \parencite{xv6}.

Bach supplies a broader Unix-kernel account. Stevens and Rago's third
edition supplies the application-facing distinction between standardized
interfaces and implementation behavior. Stevens, Fenner, and Rudoff
supply the sockets and stream-programming background. These works cover
different layers and periods; their examples are not interchangeable
snapshots of a single operating system \parencite{bach,apue,unp}.

The FFS paper is used both historically and technically.
Its measured results belong to its tested machines and workloads.
Its general lesson about locality and abstraction is a synthesis here,
not a claim that the same performance ratio applies to modern storage
\parencite{ffs}.

\section{Tools, criticism, and the screen}

Roddy's guide and Cherry and Lesk's macro documentation explain the roff
family and the boundary between primitive requests and document structure.
The supplied Cherry--Lesk text identifies the Research Tenth Edition
context but does not establish a precise date; the bibliography retains
that uncertainty \parencite{roddy,cherry-lesk}.

The Kernighan--Pike file used here is a Russian translation with an original
English publication date of 1984 and a translation date of 2003.
It supports the account of an integrated programming environment.
The shell examples in this manuscript are original and are not presented
as quotations or translations from that file \parencite{programming-env}.

Raymond's supplied printing bears a 2004 copyright although the work was
first published in 2003. Its design rules are discussed as advice to be
tested, not as a binding definition of Unix. The
\emph{UNIX-HATERS Handbook} supplies counterevidence about usability and
integration. The 1990 random-input study supplies measured criticism
with an explicitly limited sample. None of those sources licenses an
unsupported claim about the reliability of every current system
\parencite{raymond,haters,fuzz}.

The Mach paper describes a 1986 implementation and research direction.
Later microkernel developments and Apple's XNU are not collapsed into
that early design. The distinction is essential because a project's
name can survive substantial architectural change \parencite{mach}.

\section{Modern material and the limits of this account}

The supplied historical collection does not fully cover contemporary
Wayland, Android, containers, or Proton. The modern chapters therefore
add established systems knowledge and explicitly identified official
project documentation. The Wayland project overview establishes the
protocol's compositor, input, and buffer model. Bionic's maintainer guide
establishes its library and loader roles. Proton's documentation establishes
its Wine-based purpose and the existence of version-specific configuration
and component integration \parencite{wayland,bionic,proton}.

The cgroup v2 documentation supplies the resource-control model.
These online references were consulted on 6 November 2026.
Where available, the bibliography records the fetched document's Git blob
identifier. A blob identifies file content; it is not a claim to have
pinned an entire project release. URLs may later show newer content
\parencite{cgroups}.

Detailed explanations of graphics synchronization, Android lifecycle,
and distributed failure are the author's technical synthesis.
They should not be mistaken for measurements conducted for this book
or quotations from the historical collection. The account deliberately
avoids current market-share figures, a list of supported phone models,
and universal game-compatibility promises.

Historical precision includes knowing when evidence is incomplete.
Rather than smoothing every ambiguity into a confident date or claiming
all branches developed in isolation, this book preserves distinctions
that affect the explanation. The aim is a history readers can question,
trace, and extend, not a legend that depends on never opening its sources.
